---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DotPlot from '../components/DotPlot.astro';
import LineChart from '../components/LineChart.astro';
import ChainsTable from '../components/ChainsTable.astro';
import ProductsTable from '../components/ProductsTable.astro';
import WideTable from '../components/WideTable.astro';
import MiddleTable from '../components/MiddleTable.astro';
import { ladderRuns, progress, TIER_LABELS, type Experiment } from '../lib/data';
import { pct } from '../lib/format';

// Colour follows the run, in the order runs were added (at most four on one chart).
const runs = ladderRuns().slice(-4);
const colors = ['var(--series-2)', 'var(--series-1)', 'var(--series-3)', 'var(--series-4)'];
const colorOf = (e: Experiment) => colors[runs.indexOf(e)] ?? 'var(--muted)';
const latest = runs[runs.length - 1];
const ladder = progress.ladder;
const won = latest ? ladder.filter((t) => t.startsWith('T') && latest.groups[t]?.won).length : 0;
const tiersTotal = ladder.filter((t) => t.startsWith('T')).length;
const withCurves = runs.filter((e) => e.curve.length > 0);
const loopBodies = ['sum a..a+n-1', 'a^n', 'fib(a,a)', 'x->3x+1', 'acc=(acc<<1)^a'];
const loopKinds = ['accumulate', 'repeat', 'recurrence'];
const lengths = ['n15', 'n63', 'n255'];
const cell = (e: Experiment | undefined, name: string) => e?.families[name]?.exact ?? null;
---

<Layout
  title="Results: in-context arithmetic at 16 and 32 bits"
  description="How well Teenie infers unseen integer functions at 16 and 32 bits, against lookup baselines: multi-step chains, products and calibrated confidence."
  keywords={['benchmark', 'lookup baseline', 'calibrated confidence', 'out-of-distribution generalization', '32-bit arithmetic']}
  kind="article"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Results</p>
  <h1>Where the model stands</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Everything here is measured at 16 and 32 bits, where a lucky guess is one in 65,536 or one in four billion and
    the 999 examples cover a vanishing fraction of all inputs — so a right answer has to come from inferring the
    relation. Scores are shown next to lookup methods that understand nothing, and with how often the model is sure
    and right.
  </p>

  <h2>Rules with no textbook solution</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    37 held-out relations with a definite rule but no obvious way to work it out from examples, at 32 bits. The score
    is <strong>bits recovered</strong>: 32 + log<sub>2</sub> of the probability the model gives the true answer, so 0
    is a blind guess, 32 is certain and right, and 10 means the true answer is about 1,000 times more likely than by
    chance. The fitted baselines are gradient-boosted trees (one per answer bit) and a small neural network trained on
    each task's own 999 examples and calibrated on held-back ones; Teenie reads the examples in one pass and is never
    trained on any of these rules. Green: ahead of every baseline by more than half a bit; red: behind.
  </p>
  <MiddleTable />
  <p class="small muted">
    Teenie wins wherever the rule needs real computation (carries, products, automaton steps, substitution tables),
    is narrowly ahead on random expressions, and loses on rotations, which it had never trained on; fitted trees read a rotated bit
    directly. Hashes and many mixing rounds score about zero for everyone, as they must. Not yet compared: TabPFN.
  </p>

  <h2>Earlier model: unseen relations against lookup</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    The width-free model after its 24- and 32-bit stage. Lookup is the best of five methods (closest example, or matching
    one or two neighbouring bits), chosen per relation after the fact.
  </p>
  <WideTable />

  <h2>Multi-step chains</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    A start followed by one or two of 21 kinds of step, with fresh random constants; beyond lookup counts only questions
    every lookup method gets wrong.
  </p>
  <ChainsTable />

  <h2>Learning to compute through a product</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    Held-out product chains at 16 bits as training went on. Matching examples cannot get past about bit 5 of a product;
    scratch registers were the first change that let the model compute through it.
  </p>
  <ProductsTable />
  <p class="small muted">At 32 bits these are still at zero: the product skill reaches about bit 15 of a 32-bit number so far.</p>

  <h2 id="ladder">Earlier: the difficulty ladder</h2>
  <p>
    Before the move to wide numbers, every run was scored on a ladder of test tiers — random expressions of 2, 3–4 and
    5–7 operations with held-out combinations, new operation types and loops — mostly at 8 bits. At 8 bits the examples
    cover 1.5% of all inputs and lookup methods reach about a third of the sealed set, so these scores overstate what
    the model inferred; they are kept as the record. Each run is scored at the width in its title (8 bits unless
    stated). A tier is <b>won</b> at ≥50% exact with its sure answers right ≥95% of the time.
  </p>

  {
    latest && (
      <div class="grid-3 stats">
        <div class="card stat">
          <span class="label">Tiers won by the latest run</span>
          <span class="value">
            {won} of {tiersTotal}
          </span>
          <span class="note">{latest.title}</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card stat">
          <span class="label">Hidden compositions</span>
          <span class="value">{pct(latest.groups.hidden?.exact)}</span>
          <span class="note">never generated for training</span>
        </div>
        <div class="card stat">
          <span class="label">Unseen loop bodies (T5a)</span>
          <span class="value">{pct(latest.groups.T5a?.exact)}</span>
          <span class="note">{`at the trained length; loops run longer than trained (T5b, a stretch): ${pct(latest.groups.T5b?.exact)}`}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    )
  }

  <h2>Scoreboard</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <DotPlot
      title="Exact match per tier"
      reference={0.5}
      methods={runs.map((e) => ({ name: e.title, color: colorOf(e) }))}
      rows={ladder.map((t) => ({
        name: t,
        values: runs.map((e) => e.groups[t]?.exact ?? null),
        note: TIER_LABELS[t],
      }))}
    />
    <details>
      <summary>Table view</summary>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>tier</th>
              {runs.map((e) => <th class="num">{e.title}</th>)}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              ladder.map((t) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{TIER_LABELS[t]}</td>
                  {runs.map((e) => {
                    const g = e.groups[t];
                    return (
                      <td class="num">
                        <b>{pct(g?.exact)}</b> {g?.won ? '✓' : ''}
                        <span class="small muted"> sure-right {pct(g?.sure_accuracy)}</span>
                      </td>
                    );
                  })}
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
  <div class="tiers small muted">
    <p>
      <b>T1–T3</b>: random expressions of 2, 3–4 and 5–7 operations that always contain a combination never shown in
      training (a xor of a product, a product of a difference, a min of a sum, a shift of a xor). <b>T4</b>: operation
      types never trained — division, modulo, rotations, conditionals. <b>T5a</b>: loop bodies never generated (repeat
      count from b, up to 15 as in training) and unseen bit loops — recognising a new loop. <b>T5b</b>: loops run 63 or 255
      times, longer than anything trained — repeating a step further than trained, a research stretch. <b>Product, then a step</b>: a*b+b, a*b+5, a*b−a, (a*b)^a.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    withCurves.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2>During training</h2>
        <p>Mean exact match per tier on fixed validation episodes (128 questions per relation), every 500 updates.</p>
        <div class="grid-2">
          {ladder.filter((t) => withCurves.some((e) => e.curve.some((p) => typeof p[t] === 'number'))).map((t) => (
            <div class="card">
              <p class="eyebrow">{TIER_LABELS[t]}</p>
              <LineChart
                title={`${t} during training`}
                xLabel="training updates"
                height={180}
                reference={0.5}
                series={withCurves
                  .map((e) => ({
                    name: e.title,
                    color: colorOf(e),
                    points: e.curve.filter((p) => typeof p[t] === 'number').map((p) => ({ x: p.update, y: p[t]! })),
                  }))
                  .filter((series) => series.points.length > 0)}
              />
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </>
    )
  }

  {
    latest && (
      <>
        <h2>Loops in detail</h2>
        <p>
          Latest run, exact match. Training only ever used repeat counts up to 15; 63 and 255 are longer loops. Some loops
          have short closed forms (the sum is a·n + n(n−1)/2) — finding either a procedure or a shortcut counts.
        </p>
        <div class="card table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>loop</th>
                {lengths.map((l) => <th class="num">count ≤ {l.slice(1)}</th>)}
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {loopBodies.map((body) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>{body}</code> <span class="small muted">hidden body</span>
                  </td>
                  {lengths.map((l) => (
                    <td class="num">{pct(cell(latest, `${body} ${l}`))}</td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))}
              {loopKinds.map((kind) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <code>{kind}</code> <span class="small muted">random bodies</span>
                  </td>
                  {lengths.map((l) => (
                    <td class="num">{pct(cell(latest, `${kind} ${l}`))}</td>
                  ))}
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    )
  }

</Layout>

<style>
  .stats {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .tiers p {
    max-width: none;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
